{"name":"Flame Angelfish","hex":"#fd4c29","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible part of fire; a stream of burning vapour or gas, emitting light and heat.","A romantic partner or lover in a usually short-lived but passionate affair."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brilliant reddish orange-gold colour, like that of a flame."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flame"},{"term":"angelfish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the genus Pterophyllum of freshwater, tropical cichlids.","Any marine fish of the family Pomacanthidae, common on shallow tropical reefs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/angelfish"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flame angelfish","blocks":[{"text":"The flame angelfish is a marine angelfish of the family Pomacanthidae found in tropical waters of the Pacific Ocean. Other common names include flame angel, flaming angelfish and Japanese pygmy angelfish."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Flame_angelfish_%28Centropyge_loricula%29.jpg/330px-Flame_angelfish_%28Centropyge_loricula%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame_angelfish","wikidata":"Q1426988"},{"title":"Flame","blocks":[{"text":"A flame is the visible, gaseous part of a fire. It is caused by a highly exothermic chemical reaction made in a thin zone. When flames are hot enough to have ionized gaseous components of sufficient density, they are then considered plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/DancingFlames.jpg/330px-DancingFlames.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame","wikidata":"Q235544"},{"title":"Scarlet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Scarlet is a bright red color, sometimes with a slightly orange tinge. In the spectrum of visible light, and on the traditional color wheel, it is one-quarter of the way between red and orange, slightly less orange than vermilion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/RCMP-female-officer.jpg/330px-RCMP-female-officer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarlet_(color)","wikidata":"Q2268159"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg/500px-Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg/1280px-Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg/500px-Centropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In Tuvalu","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACentropyge_loriculus_370210419.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/1280px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png/500px-Spectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spectrum of the blue (premixed, i.e., complete combustion) flame from a butane torch showing molecular radical band emission and Swan bands. Virtually all the light produced is in the blue to green region of the spectrum below about 565 nanometers, accounting for the bluish color of sootless hydrocarbon flames.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpectrum_of_blue_flame_-_intensity_corrected.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/1280px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg/500px-Bunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different flame types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Candlespace.jpg/500px-Candlespace.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In zero-G, convection does not carry the hot combustion products away from the fuel source, resulting in a spherical flame front.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACandlespace.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Nelson_Tower_in_Scarlet_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5188743.jpg/500px-Nelson_Tower_in_Scarlet_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5188743.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Nelson_Tower_in_Scarlet_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5188743.jpg/500px-Nelson_Tower_in_Scarlet_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5188743.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANelson_Tower_in_Scarlet_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5188743.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Anagallis_arvensis_2.jpg/500px-Anagallis_arvensis_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Anagallis_arvensis_2.jpg/500px-Anagallis_arvensis_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnagallis_arvensis_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Scarlet_ibis_arp.jpg/500px-Scarlet_ibis_arp.jpg?utm_sou